## 1. Introduction
The increasing energy demands of human science and industry call for efficient and clean energy storage and conversation devices [1–4]. The supercapacitors and secondary batteries, such as lithium sulfur battery and lithium ion battery have attracted intensive attentions [5–7]. Supercapacitors with the merits of long cycling stability, high power density, and good safety have been considered as promising candidates for the advanced energy storage devices [8,9]. However, the capacitance of traditional supercapacitors based on carbon materials is limited by the electrical double-layer mechanism [10]. Therefore, pseudocapacitive materials such as transition metal hydroxides/oxides/phosphides [11–13], metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [14–16] and conducting polymers [17] have been explored for high-performance supercapacitors in recent years.
Among the pseudocapacitive materials, the MOFs with redox-active metal ions and tuned pore structures have been frequently utilized for energy storage. In 2012, Han et al. [18] prepared the Co based MOFs by cobalt nitrate and terephthalic acid in 1 M LiOH electrolyte; the as-made Co-MOF electrode showed \(207\,\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\) at a current density of \(0.6\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) . However, the performance of CoMOF electrode was still not satisfactory for the development of supercapacitors. For improving the electrochemical performance, one of the effective strategies is doping the MOFs with the second metal ion. For instance, Wei et al. [19] introduced Zn ions into the Ni-MOF to tune the electronic properties; the fabricated Zn-doped Ni-MOF yielded a good specific capacitance of \(1620\,\mathrm{F\,g^{-1}}\) at a current density of \(0.25\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) . Co ions were also used to partially substitute Ni ions in the Ni-MOFs to increase the conductivity, the mixed-metal MOFs showed the improved electrochemical performance in comparison with the pristine Ni-MOFs.
Engineering the MOFs into two-dimensional (2D) structure is another essential method to promote the electrochemical performance of supercapacitors. The 2D MOFs are very appealing electrode materials in virtue of several advantages: (1) the in-plane charge delocalization and the extended pi-conjugation of 2D MOFs can offer high electronic conductivity; (2) the 2D structure can provide large ion-accessible surface to shorten the diffusion distance of ion transport; (3) the large aspect ratios of 2D MOFs can provide sufficient redox active sites to increase the pseudocapacitance [20– 22]. For example, 2D MOFs based on the nickel nitrate and hexaaminobenzene ligand prepared by Bao et al. [23] exhibited a high volumetric capacitance of \(760\ \mathrm{F\cm}^{-3}\) and maintained \(90\%\) initial capacitance after 12,000 cycles. Pang et al. [24] prepared ultrathin -MOF nanosheets via a facile hydrothermal synthesis, which delivered a specific capacitance of \(1169\ \mathrm{F\g^{-1}}\) at \(0.5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) . Benefiting from the merits of 2D structure, the electrochemical performance of Co-MOF nanosheets was significantly enhanced over the bulk Co-MOFs. However, as one kind of the 2D materials, the MOF belts have been rarely reported for supercapacitors.
Herein, we report a MXene-induce method to fabricate the NiMOF microbelts. MXenes are a fascinating class of 2D transition metal carbides/carbonitrides with high conductivity, tunable surface chemistry and good stability [25,26]. Due to these advantages, MXenes are directly used or to hybridize inorganic compounds to improve electrochemical performance for energy storage and conversion [27–30]. In this paper, MXenes are used not only as substrate materials to couple Ni-MOFs but also as the structure-directing agents to tune the morphology of Ni-MOFs into 2D microbelts. Moreover, the high surface area of MXenes can provide additional electric-double layer capacitance, and functional groups (such as −F and −OH) on the surface can be used as redox sites to offer faradaic pseudocapacitance [31]. Furthermore, the 2D microbelts with large aspect ratio can provide abundant reactive sites and shorten the ion-transport diffusion distance. With the syner
gistic effects of 2D Ni-MOF microbelts and MXenes, the as-made MXene/Ni-MOF composite shows a high performance of \(1124\,\mathrm{~F~}\) \(\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\) with good rate capability as well as cycling stability.
## 2. Experimental and characterization
## 2.1. Synthesis of Ni-MOF and \(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}/N i-M O F\)
\(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}\) was prepared according to the previous work [32]. \(2{\mathrm{~g~}}\) MAX was added into HF solution consisting of \(40\,\mathrm{\ml\9\M\HCl}\) and \(3.2\ \mathrm{g}\) LiF, after string for \(48\;\mathrm{h}\) , the resulting mixture was then centrifugated until \(\mathsf{p H}\) value approximately to 7 to obtain dark green \(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}\) MXenes. To prepared Ni-MOFs, 1.5 mmol \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) \(6\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) and 1 mmol terephthalic acid were added in the mixture of \(30\;\mathrm{ml}\;\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}\) -dimethylformamide and \(5\mathrm{~ml~H}_{2}0\) , after ultrasonication for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) the fully mixed solution was refluxed for \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) under Ar protection, followed by centrifugating with ethanol and freeze drying. As for \(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}/\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) , the \(40\,\mathrm{\mg\Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\) was additionally added at the beginning and followed the same with the process of Ni-MOFs. The mass content of MXenes in \(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}/N i-M O F s\) is around \(30\%\) .
## 2.2. Characterization
X-ray diffraction (Shimadzu X-ray diffractometer) patterns were collected to observe crystalline structures of samples. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (ESCALAB250Xi) was recorded to probe the chemical composition of the samples. The field-emission scanning electron microscope (FEI, Nova NanoSEM 450) and transmission electron microscope (JEM-F200) were used to explore the morphologies. Fourier-transform infrared spectrometer (Nicolet INTO MX & IS10) was employed to analyze the functional groups. The physical sorption instrument (Autosorb-iQ-C) was used to determine the surface areas of samples.
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
Three electrode tests were measured by the CHI 760E electrochemistry workstation. The working electrodes were prepared by the active materials, carbon black and polyvinylidene fluoride with mass ratio of 7:2:1. In addition, a Hg HgO electrode and Pt foil was used as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The electrodes were tested in 6 M KOH aqueous electrolyte. The capacitance C \((\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) was calculated by the galvanostatic discharge curves.
\[
C=\frac{\mathrm{I}\Delta\mathrm{t}}{\mathrm{m}\Delta\mathrm{V}}
\]
where \(\Delta\mathbf{t}\) , I and m represents the discharge time (s), the discharging current (A), and the mass of active material in a single electrode (g), respectively.
## 2.4. Fabrication of the asymmetric supercapacitor
\(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}/N i-M O F s\) (as the positive electrode) and active carbon (as the negative electrode) were used to assemble the asymmetric supercapacitors. The charge balance can be controlled by the mass loading of the two electrodes. The mass ratio between the positive \((\mathbf{m}^{+})\) and negative \((\mathbf{m}^{-})\) active material is determined via \({\mathsf{C}}^{+}{\mathsf{m}}^{+}{\mathsf{=}}{\mathsf{C}}^{-}{\mathsf{m}}^{-}\) , where \(C^{+}\) and \(C^{-}\) are the capacitances of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs and active carbon measured in three electrode system. The cyclic voltammetry and galvanostatic charge–discharge were tested in an electrochemical workstation (CHI 760E) and the cycle performance was measured by the LAND battery test system (CT2001A). The energy density (E, Wh \(\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\) ) and power density (P, W \(\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\) ) were calculated by the equations:
\[
\mathbf{E}=\mathbf{CV}^2/2
\]
\[
\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{E}/\mathrm{t},
\]
where C,V and t is the specific capacitance of the asymmetric supercapacitor \((\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), the potential (V) and the galvanostatic discharge time (s), respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of Ni-MOF and \(T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}/N i-M O F\)
Fig. 1 illustrates the synthetic process of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\) Ni-MOFs. The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes are able to induce the construction of Ni-MOF microbelts; however, without the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes, the free-growing Ni-MOFs become agglomerated nanosheets with indistinct outline. Fig. 2 shows the morphology of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}-\) MOFs by the field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). The Ni-MOFs without MXenes show the undivided 2D nanosheets assembled with each other to form the bulk structure. After introducing the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXene of a few microns width and around \(2.5\,\textrm{n m}\) thickness (Figs. S1 and S2), the layered microbelts with the width of about \(1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) can be found for the Ni-MOFs. The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3^{-}}\) \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs microbelts are more dispersed than Ni-MOFs nanosheets. It is interesting to find that, when the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes were replaced with graphene oxides, which are 2D material bearing oxygen functional groups on the surface similar to MXenes,
the mcriosheets with larger width and shorter length could be obtained (Fig. S3). This phenomenon indicates that both the functional groups and 2D structure of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes can induce the formation of Ni-MOFs microbelts.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was utilized to further observe the morphology of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs. As shown in Fig. 3, the layered microbelt structure can be clearly seen for Ni-MOFs, and the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes are coupled with Ni-MOFs. Highresolution TEM (HR-TEM, Fig. S4) images further show the interfaces between the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes and Ni-MOFs, indicating successful preparation of the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs composites. Notably, the originally smooth \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes became wrinkled (Fig. S1), suggesting large number of defect sites created during the reaction. The scanning TEM and corresponding elemental mapping images (Fig. 3d) show the presence of the elements C, O, Ni and Ti, confirming the formation of the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs composites.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) was used to analyze the crystal structure of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs. According to Fig. 4a, the Ni-MOFs show peaks at \(9.1^{\circ}\), \(11.8^{\circ}\), \(15.6^{\circ}\), \(18.4^{\circ}\), \(23.8^{\circ}\) and \(28.8^{\circ}\) from the (100), (010), (10−1), (200), (020) and \((-1-21)\) faces, respectively; these peaks are almost unchanged after introduction of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes. The characteristic peaks of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes (Fig. S5) cannot be observed, indicating that the MXenes are further exfoliated to thinner layers during the reaction and the tacking of MXene sheets is suppressed [33]. In addition, the mass content of MXenes in \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is around \(30\%\), and the peaks of MXenes can be overlapped by those of Ni-MOFs. Fig. 4b
presents the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs. For the Ni-MOFs, the characteristic peak at \(816~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be belonged to the aromatic deformation vibration (in plane) of C-H bond. The peaks appeared at 1015, 1096 and \(1145~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are for the C-H of aromatic deformation vibration (out plane). Moreover, the peaks at about \(1377\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is attributed to the carboxyl groups, and those at 1497 and \(1577\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) originate from the \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) of stretching vibration. All the above signals belong to the terephthalic acid ligands. The additional broad band around \(3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is associated with the absorbed \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules [15]. The bands of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs are nearly the same as those of NiMOFs, confirming their similar components. The band of C-F species in \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is almost undetected, which may be covered by aromatic deformation vibration of C-H groups.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) shows that the NiMOFs contain elements of C, O and Ni while the additional signals of Ti and F elements can be found in \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs (Fig. S6a). The C1s spectrum of Ni-MOFs (Fig. S6b) shows peaks for \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) species \((284.6~\mathrm{eV})\) and the carbon in carboxyl groups \((288.3~\mathrm{eV})\) [34], which is referring to the terephthalic acid ligands; the additional peak of C-Ti bond can be found at \(281.5~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs, indicating the existence of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes [33]. Moreover, the Ni 2p spectrum of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs (Fig. 5a) can be fitted into two spin–orbit doublet peaks at 873.6 and \(855.9~\mathrm{eV}\) with two shakeup satellite peaks at about 879.5 and \(861.4\,\mathrm{eV}\) , indicating the presence of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) from the Ni-MOFs [35]. The Ni-MOFs show the similar Ni 2p spectrum. It's worth noting that the Ni 2p signals of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) - MOFs slightly shifted to the lower binding positions compared to
those of Ni-MOFs, indicative of the charge transfer and strong interactions between \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) and Ni-MOFs.
Fig. 5b delivers the nitrogen adsorption/desorption isotherms of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\). The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) show a slightly higher Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area of \(96.6\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\) \(\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) than Ni-MOFs \((84.4\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1})\), indicating that the introduction of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes can increase surface area due to the thin layers of exfoliated MXenes and the dispersed Ni-MOFs microbelts. The high surface area may provide more active sites to improve electrochemical performance.
## 3.2. Electrochemical evaluation
The electrochemical characteristics of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}-\) MOFs were first carried out in a three-electrode test system. As can be seen from Figs. 6a-b and S7, both the samples exhibit typical cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves with redox couple peaks for faradaic pseudocapacitance, but the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) exhibits larger CV areas (Fig. 6a-b) in comparison with those of Ni-MOFs, suggesting that \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni-MOFs}\) has higher specific capacitance and better rate capability. The galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD)
curves of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs at current density of 1 A \(\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) are presented in Fig. 6c. The GCD curves of both samples are nonlinear and asymmetric, confirming the pseudocapacitive characteristics. The specific capacitances of Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) - MOFs at current densities calculated by the discharge profiles were summarized in Fig. 6d. The Ni-MOFs show a specific capacitance of \(786\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , while that of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is \(1124\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , significantly higher that the former. Meanwhile, the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) - MOFs retain a large specific capacitance of \(697\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at the current density of \(20\ \mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , corresponding to \(62\%\) capacitance retention. The rate capability of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is better than that of NiMOFs \((41\%)\) . Fig. S8 shows the GCD curves and the specific capacitances of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes at different discharge current densities. The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes exhibit \(100\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and maintain \(80\%\) capacitance retention at the current density of \(20\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . The specific capacitance of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is much larger than those of MXenes and Ni-MOFs, indicating that the synergistic effects of 2D Ni-MOF microbelts and MXenes can boost the specific capacitance. The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs also possess a lower charge-transfer resistance than that of Ni-MOFs due to the smaller diameter of the semicircle as shown in the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy plots (Fig. 6e). For further detailed analysis, the equivalent circuit is shown in Fig. S9, where \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{s}},\,\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{d}}\) and \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) is internal resistance, the double layer capacity and charge transfer resistance, respectively. \(\mathrm{Q_{A}}\) represent the adsorption of \(\mathrm{OH^{-}}\) and \(\mathrm{Q}_{o}\) is constant phase element. The values of these elements are summarized in Table S1. \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs electrode has a much lower \(\mathrm{R_{ct}}\) \((9.35\,\mathrm{\Ohm})\) than that of Ni-MOFs \((30.01\,\mathrm{\Ohm})\) . In addition, the \(\mathrm{Q}_{o}\) of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs is 0.76, higher than that of Ni-MOFs (0.58), and closer to 1, indicating the surface controlled electrochemical behavior [36]. This surface-defined behavior can enhance reaction kinetics for the redox reactions to boost the pseudocapacitances of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs [37]. The long cycling performances of the Ni-MOFs and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs were further performed at \(10\,\mathrm{A}\) \(\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) . The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs maintained \(83.2\%\) capacitance retention after 4000 cycles while there was a \(50.4\%\) decay for Ni-MOFs. The 2D belt structure of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs can be still remained to a certain degree after 4000 cycles (Fig. S10), suggesting good structure stability. The high performance of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs may be because the 2D miccobelts can offer rich redox sites and reduce the ion transport distance. Furthermore, the presence of exfoliated \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes can improve the electrical conductivity of composites and provide extra electric-double layer and faradaic capacitances
The asymmetric supercapacitor was fabricated with \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) - MOFs and activated carbon (AC) as the cathode and anode materials, respectively. It can be found from Fig. S11 that AC shows rectangular CV curves, indicative of the electric-double layer capacitance behavior. The CV curves of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs//AC full cells in different voltage windows are displayed in Fig. S12. It can be found in a high voltage window \((+1.6\mathrm{~V})\) , the curves are distorted, meaning the electrolyte hydrolyzes seriously. Therefore, the \(0{-}1.6\mathrm{~V~}\) range as the maximized stable window can be used for the electrochemical testing. Fig. 7a shows quasi-rectangular CV curves for the asymmetric supercapacitor, indicating that the capacitance of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs//AC is contributed by capacitive character and pseudocapacitive behavior. The asymmetric triangular GCD curves (Fig. 7b) of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs//AC reveal similar results as the CV curves. As shown in Fig. 7c, the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) - MOFs//AC exhibits a specific capacitance of \(128\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(1\ \mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ; when the current density is increased to \(10\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , the asymmetric supercapacitor can still reach \(65\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) ( \(51\%\) capacitance retention). The energy densities and power densities are summarized in the Ragone plot as shown in Fig. 7d. The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs//AC gives a high energy density of \(46\,\mathrm{~W~h~}\,\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at the power density of \(0.8\,\mathrm{\kW\kg^{-1}}\) , and it shows the energy density of \(24\ \mathrm{Wh}\ \mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at
the high power density of \(8\,\mathrm{\kW\kg^{-1}}\) . The \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs//AC supercapacitor also has a good cyclic stability with \(74.8\%\) capacitance retention after 4000 cycles at \(10\,\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . These results suggest their potential application in real supercapacitors.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we designed a MXene-induce method to fabricate the Ni-MOF microbelts. The oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface and the 2D structure of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes are critical factors for the formation of microbelts. The 2D miccobelts are able to expose rich redox sites and reduce the ion transport path. The introduction of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes can guarantee fast ion transfer and increase the capacitances. With the synergistic effects of 2D Ni-MOF microbelts and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}\) MXenes, the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}/\mathrm{Ni}\) -MOFs based supercapacitor shows a high capacitance of \(1124\;\mathrm{F}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at a current density of \(1\ \mathrm{A}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with \(62\%\) retention of \(697\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at 20 \(\mathrm{A}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . This research may provide a potential design strategy to prepare 2D MOF belts for energy storage applications.
